From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Birk Skyum <birk.skyum@pm.me>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 15:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: Add the Samsung Galaxy Book Go 5G

Add the Samsung Galaxy Book Go 5G (NP545XLA), a Snapdragon 8cx Gen 2
(SC8180XP) clamshell. Its DSDT is Qualcomm's CLS board with board ID 3,
so the modem subsystem reports CLSC8180, the integrated WLAN is off and
the Wi-Fi card sits on PCIe 0, as on the Dell Inspiron 14 3420. Samsung
adds its own embedded controller and the keyboard and touchpad that the
7c Galaxy Book Go also has.

The GPIO interrupts in this DSDT use GIO0 virtual pins. Pin 64 * n is
the nth interrupt in GIO0's _CRS, and the PDC routes that interrupt from
one GPIO. Read that way, the Flex 5G's touchpad (pin 640) and PCIe 3
wake (pin 576) come out as GPIO 24 and 180, which its tree uses, and
this laptop's lid event (pin 320) as GPIO 50, the pin GIO0 reads as
LIDR.

The DSDT gives these values.

- The keyboard (SSEC0001, \_SB.I2C9.ECKB) is HID over I2C at 0x5 on the
  QUP at 0xa80000 (i2c8) at 400 kHz. Its _DSM puts the HID descriptor
  at 0x20, and its interrupt is pin 640, GSIV 605, GPIO 125, level low.
- The touchpad (SSTP0001, \_SB.I2C1.TCPD) is at 0x40 on the QUP at
  0x880000 (i2c0) at 400 kHz, with its descriptor at 0xe. Its interrupt
  is pin 448, GSIV 590, GPIO 104, level low, and the PEP powers it from
  LDO11_C at 3.3 V.
- The PEP muxes GPIO 0 and 1 to qup0 and GPIO 88 and 89 to qup8 with
  pull-ups. GPIO 0, 1 and 50 are the OS's here, so only 2 to 3, 47 to
  49 and 126 to 129 of the Flex 5G's reserved ranges stay.
- The lid (LID0) reads GPIO 50, and GIO0's _AEI watches it on both
  edges.
- PRP0 and PRP3 enable PCIe 0 and 3 only. QPPX lists the reset GPIOs 35
  and 178, the PEP sets CLKREQ on GPIO 36 and 179, and the root ports
  wake on pins 384 and 576, GPIO 37 and 180. PCIe 0 holds the Wi-Fi
  card, which the PEP powers from S5A, S6C and S7C and enables with
  GPIO 169, as on the Inspiron.
- STOR is 1, so the drive is UFS. The PEP's UFS, USB and PCIe PHY rails
  are the Flex 5G DSDT's, so the regulators and their ranges are the
  Flex 5G's.
- The GPU SMMU's context interrupts are GSIV 712 to 719, edge
  triggered, as the Inspiron's tree has them.
- Samsung's modem subsystem INF installs qcmpss8180_nm.mbn for
  CLSC8180.

From the Inspiron 14 3420's tree come the 3.07 and 3.15 GHz OPPs of the
prime cores, the GPU SMMU interrupts and the RTC offset in UEFI. The
memory carveouts and rmtfs are the Flex 5G's, which the Inspiron's UEFI
memory map matches.

The tree leaves these out.

- The display. The panel is eDP, powered from GPIO 130 in GPU0's PEP,
  with a PMIC PWM backlight whose LPG channel the panel config doesn't
  name. MDSS stays off and the screen on the firmware's framebuffer.
- Wi-Fi and Bluetooth. The card on PCIe 0 is a QCA6390 (the bundle's
  qcbtfmuart_hst8180 and wlanfw20.mbn), which Linux powers through a
  qcom,qca6390-pmu node. The DSDT doesn't say which of S5A, S6C and S7C
  feeds which PMU input, so there is none, and the card stays as the
  firmware leaves it.
- USB-C roles and orientation. UCS0 lists no orientation or SBU mux
  GPIOs, so the three USB controllers are host only, as on the
  Inspiron.
- Audio, the cameras, the SD card slot, the SAR sensors and the EC's
  other devices (SAM060B at 0x62 on i2c1, EMEC, UCME).

The firmware loads from qcom/sc8180xp/Samsung/galaxy-book-go-5g. The
ADSP image is signed under Samsung's root, the CDSP, modem and GPU zap
shader images under Qualcomm's QMC Attestation Root CA 3. Not booted on
the laptop yet.

Signed-off-by: Birk Skyum <birk.skyum@pm.me>
